---
<Layout title="Research | Zhiqian Chen">
  <main class="container">
    <Nav current="research" />

    <section class="hero">
      <h1>Research Statement</h1>
      <div class="hero-grid">
      <p class="lead">COVID-19 has caused over 6 million deaths worldwide since late 2019 and an estimated $12.5 trillion in economic loss as of 2024. The pandemic exposed deep vulnerabilities in modern networked systems: disrupted supply chains, financial market volatility, misinformation amplified across social networks. The July 2024 CrowdStrike update failure brought airlines, hospitals, and businesses to a standstill worldwide, underscoring how far cascading effects travel inside interconnected systems. Climate change, pandemics, and food security all run on interdependent networks (transportation, finance, supply chains, ecology, social networks) that form the backbone of our societal and economic infrastructure. As the world grows more interconnected, resilient and interpretable network models become essential.</p>
      <blockquote class="statement">
        <strong>Research interest.</strong> I explore the fundamental principles of networked system dynamics by integrating machine learning, network science, and dynamical systems theory. The work spans theoretical innovation and practical application, and centers on efficient, interpretable models that improve decision-making in real-world contexts.
      </blockquote>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section overview" id="overview">
      <h2>At a glance</h2>
      <p class="section-lead">Three application areas, two methods that run through all of them, and the grants that fund the work.</p>
      <div class="glance">
        <div class="glance-col">
          <h4>Application areas</h4>
          <a class="glance-item" href="#line-1"><span class="interest-num">1</span>Network science, graph machine learning and dynamical systems</a>
          <a class="glance-item" href="#line-2"><span class="interest-num">2</span>Mechanistic understanding of large language models</a>
          <a class="glance-item" href="#line-3"><span class="interest-num">3</span>Computational musicology</a>
        </div>
        <div class="glance-col">
          <h4>Methods</h4>
          <a class="glance-item" href="#method-uq"><span class="interest-num">A</span>Uncertainty quantification</a>
          <a class="glance-item" href="#method-spectral"><span class="interest-num">B</span>Spectral analysis, mainly on graphs</a>
        </div>
        <div class="glance-col">
          <h4>Funding</h4>
          {externalGrants.slice(0, 4).map((g) => (
            <a class="glance-item glance-grant" href="#funding"><span class="glance-agency">{g.agency}</span>{g.title.split(':')[0]} {g.awardNo}</a>
          ))}
          <a class="glance-more" href="#funding">All {externalGrants.length} external and {internalSupport.length} internal awards →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="research-lines">
      <h2>Application areas</h2>
      <p class="section-lead">The work runs along three lines, in order of weight. The first is the long-standing core. The second and third are newer and growing.</p>

      <h3 class="interest-head" id="line-1"><span class="interest-num">1</span>Network science, graph machine learning and dynamical systems</h3>
      <div class="interest-body">
        <p>Roads, power grids, epidemics and social media are all networks, and what happens on one network often spreads into another. I study how behavior spreads, settles and breaks in connected systems, and how to estimate and control it.</p>
        <p>I look for generalized patterns that hold across very different networks, and for cross-system behaviors that pass from one network to another. The tools are spectral and functional analysis and uncertainty quantification. The systems studied include traffic grids, power networks, epidemics, social networks, banking and supply chains.</p>
        <p>One example is a real-time Manhattan power and traffic co-simulation, in which electric vehicle charging load is fed back into the grid. Relevant support: <a href="https://www.nsf.gov/awardsearch/showAward?AWD_ID=2443266" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NSF CAREER #2443266</a> and <a href="https://www.nsf.gov/awardsearch/showAward?AWD_ID=2345921" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NSF CIRC #2345921</a>, both listed in the funding sections below.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="thrust-row">
        {thrustImgs.map((t) => (
          <figure class="thrust-item">
            {t.href ? (
              <a href={t.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label={t.alt}><img src={t.src} alt={t.alt} loading="lazy" /></a>
            ) : (
              <img src={t.src} alt={t.alt} loading="lazy" />
            )}
            <figcaption><strong>{t.cap}</strong><span>{t.sub}</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="interest-work">
            <h4>Selected work</h4>
            <ul>
              {linkNetworks.map((w) => (
                <li>{w.href ? <a href={w.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{w.title}</a> : <span class="work-title">{w.title}</span>} <span class="work-venue">{w.venue}</span></li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>

      <h3 class="interest-head" id="line-2"><span class="interest-num">2</span>Mechanistic understanding of large language models</h3>
      <div class="interest-split">
        <figure class="interest-fig">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 530 124" role="img" aria-labelledby="llm-fig-title">
                <title id="llm-fig-title">Delta graphs, steering and stitching</title>
                <defs>
                  <marker id="arrowhead" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="5" markerHeight="5" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z" fill="#4E5C6A" /></marker>
                  <marker id="arrowhead-hot" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="5" markerHeight="5" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z" fill="#C45200" /></marker>
                </defs>
                <text x="90" y="11" class="fig-head">DELTA GRAPH</text>
                {[[15, 0], [115, 1]].map(([x0, after]) => (
                  <g>
                    {llmEdges.map(([a, b], i) => {
                      const p = llmNodes[a], q = llmNodes[b];
                      const kind = after ? (llmChanged.includes(i) ? 'hot' : llmLost.includes(i) ? 'lost' : '') : '';
                      return <line x1={x0 + p[0]} y1={p[1]} x2={x0 + q[0]} y2={q[1]} class={`fig-edge ${kind}`} />;
                    })}
                    {llmNodes.map(([x, y]) => <circle cx={x0 + x} cy={y} r="4.5" class="fig-node" />)}
                    <text x={x0 + 25} y="114" class="fig-cap">{after ? 'after edit' : 'before'}</text>
                  </g>
                ))}
                <path d="M77 58 h26" class="fig-arrow" marker-end="url(#arrowhead)" />

                <text x="275" y="11" class="fig-head">STEERING</text>
                {[30, 52, 74].map((y) => <rect x="235" y={y} width="80" height="12" class="fig-layer" />)}
                <path d="M352 58 h-33" class="fig-arrow hot" marker-end="url(#arrowhead-hot)" />
                <text x="338" y="51" class="fig-cap hot">+v</text>
                <text x="275" y="114" class="fig-cap">add a direction</text>

                <text x="450" y="11" class="fig-head">STITCHING</text>
                <rect x="420" y="26" width="60" height="12" class="fig-layer b" />
                <rect x="420" y="42" width="60" height="12" class="fig-layer b" />
                <rect x="430" y="59" width="40" height="4" class="fig-stitch" />
                <rect x="420" y="68" width="60" height="12" class="fig-layer" />
                <rect x="420" y="84" width="60" height="12" class="fig-layer" />
                <text x="486" y="44" class="fig-cap start">model B</text>
                <text x="486" y="86" class="fig-cap start">model A</text>
                <text x="450" y="114" class="fig-cap">join two models</text>
              </svg>
            </figure>
        <div class="interest-body">
          <p>Language models are changed after release. Facts are edited, layers are merged, weights are pruned or compressed. A model can pass the usual output tests after such a change while something inside it has broken.</p>
          <p>I read a trained model's activations as a graph and compare that graph before and after a change. <em>Delta-Graph Signatures</em> (SDM 2026, accepted) uses this comparison to flag knowledge edits that pass standard checks but damage unrelated knowledge. A separate CIKM 2026 paper gives a formal pre-condition for checking whether a knowledge graph structurally supports a query before a language model generates from it.</p>
          <p>Next steps are <strong>steering</strong>, <strong>model stitching</strong>, and detecting silent degradation from other model changes: fine-tuning, merging, quantization, pruning and distillation. The colloquium "From Networks to Neural Networks" gives a tutorial-style overview of this line.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="interest-work">
            <h4>Selected work</h4>
            <ul>
              {linkLLM.map((w) => (
                <li>{w.href ? <a href={w.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{w.title}</a> : <span class="work-title">{w.title}</span>} <span class="work-venue">{w.venue}</span></li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>

      <h3 class="interest-head" id="line-3"><span class="interest-num">3</span>Computational musicology</h3>
      <div class="interest-split">
        <figure class="interest-fig">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 530 124" role="img" aria-labelledby="music-fig-title">
                <title id="music-fig-title">Genre fusion, and looking inside music generation models</title>
                <text x="125" y="11" class="fig-head">GENRE FUSION, ICDM 2017</text>
                {[[20, 28, 'a', 'genre A'], [20, 76, 'b', 'genre B'], [165, 52, 'f', 'fused']].map(([x, y, tone, label]) => (
                  <g>
                    {[0, 1, 2, 3, 4].map((k) => <line x1={x} y1={y + k * 4.5} x2={x + 80} y2={y + k * 4.5} class="fig-staff" />)}
                    {musicNotes[tone].map(([dx, step]) => <ellipse cx={x + dx} cy={y + 18 - step * 2.4} rx="3.6" ry="2.7" class={`fig-note-head ${tone}`} />)}
                    <text x={x + 40} y={y + 32} class="fig-cap">{label}</text>
                  </g>
                ))}
                <path d="M104 38 C128 38 136 58 158 60" class="fig-arrow" marker-end="url(#arrowhead)" />
                <path d="M104 86 C128 86 136 66 158 64" class="fig-arrow" marker-end="url(#arrowhead)" />

                <text x="410" y="11" class="fig-head">INSIDE MUSIC MODELS</text>
                {[26, 44, 62, 80].map((y, i) => <rect x="310" y={y} width="80" height="12" class={`fig-layer ${i === 2 ? 'hot' : ''}`} />)}
                <path d="M394 68 h22" class="fig-arrow" marker-end="url(#arrowhead)" />
                <text x="422" y="64" class="fig-cap start">where style lives,</text>
                <text x="422" y="78" class="fig-cap start">how far it steers</text>
                <text x="350" y="114" class="fig-cap">music generator</text>
              </svg>
            </figure>
        <div class="interest-body">
          <p>Music generation models now produce convincing audio, yet little is known about how they represent style. My first music paper fused two genres into a new one with adversarial dual learning (ICDM 2017, with Alexander Lerch).</p>
          <p>Music is now a main and growing line of my work. The questions are how generation models encode style, how far that style can be steered, and how this can serve music for health and hearing. EEG and wearable sensors are being considered for the health side.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="interest-work">
            <h4>Selected work</h4>
            <ul>
              {linkMusic.map((w) => (
                <li>{w.href ? <a href={w.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{w.title}</a> : <span class="work-title">{w.title}</span>} <span class="work-venue">{w.venue}</span></li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="methods">
      <h2>Methods</h2>
      <p class="section-lead">Two technical interests run through all three areas. More may join them later.</p>
      <div class="scope-row">
        <figure class="scope-figure">
          <img src="/research-scope.png" alt="Two overlapping sets. Theoretical/Tool Study covers higher-order analysis, uncertainty quantification, spectral graph theory, and approximation theory. Application Study covers traffic and urban computing, spatial epidemiology, circuit and power networks, social networks, brain networks, and genetics and genomics. Graph dynamics sits in the overlap, feeding both." />
        </figure>
        <div>
          <h3 class="interest-head" id="method-uq"><span class="interest-num">A</span>Uncertainty quantification</h3>
          <div class="interest-body">
            <p>A prediction on a network is only useful if we know how far to trust it. Data are usually sparse, such as a few road sensors or one snapshot of an outbreak, and the network itself may be uncertain.</p>
            <p>I build methods that say how sure a prediction is and that make good decisions anyway. Kalman filtering paired with graph convolution forecasts traffic with error bars. Graph Bayesian optimization finds where a spread started from a single snapshot, and picks the best seed users across several linked networks. Sobol and Shapley analysis separate a node's own effect from its interactions with others.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="interest-work">
            <h4>Selected work</h4>
            <ul>
              {linkUQ.map((w) => (
                <li>{w.href ? <a href={w.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{w.title}</a> : <span class="work-title">{w.title}</span>} <span class="work-venue">{w.venue}</span></li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="interest-head" id="method-spectral"><span class="interest-num">B</span>Spectral analysis, mainly on graphs</h3>
      <div class="interest-body">
        <p>A graph's eigenvectors play the role that sine waves play for sound. They split a signal on a network into smooth and fast-changing parts, which makes filtering, comparison and theory possible.</p>
        <p>I use this view to unify graph neural networks: spectral and spatial models turn out to be two readings of the same operator, and rational approximations give sharper graph filters. Recent work applies spectral analysis mainly to graphs, from infrastructure networks to graphs read out of model activations.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="interest-work">
        <h4>Selected work</h4>
        <ul>
          {linkSpectral.map((w) => (
            <li>{w.href ? <a href={w.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{w.title}</a> : <span class="work-title">{w.title}</span>} <span class="work-venue">{w.venue}</span></li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="funding">
      <h2>Funding · External</h2>
      <div class="list">
        {externalGrants.map((g) => {
          const RowTag = g.link ? 'a' : 'article';
          return (
            <RowTag class="grant-row" href={g.link || undefined} target={g.link ? '_blank' : undefined} rel={g.link ? 'noopener' : undefined}>
              <div class="logo-box"><img src={g.logo} alt={g.agency} loading="lazy" /></div>
              <div class="grant-content">
                <h3>{g.title}</h3>
                <p class="grant-meta">{g.awardNo ? `${g.awardNo} · ` : ''}{g.period} · {g.amount} · {g.role}</p>
                <p class="grant-desc">{g.desc}</p>
              </div>
            </RowTag>
          );
        })}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Funding · Internal</h2>
      <div class="list">
        {internalSupport.map((g) => {
          const RowTag = g.link ? 'a' : 'article';
          return (
            <RowTag class="grant-row" href={g.link || undefined} target={g.link ? '_blank' : undefined} rel={g.link ? 'noopener' : undefined}>
              <div class="logo-box"><img src={g.logo} alt={g.agency} loading="lazy" /></div>
              <div class="grant-content">
                <h3>{g.title}</h3>
                <p class="grant-meta">{g.period} · {g.amount} · {g.role}</p>
                <p class="grant-desc">{g.desc}</p>
              </div>
            </RowTag>
          );
        })}
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

</Layout>
<style>
